The resistance of a uniform wire depends on its dimensions and on the material:

- $R$ is **proportional to length** $l$ — a longer wire is a longer path for the charges;
- $R$ is **inversely proportional to cross-sectional area** $A$ — a thicker wire offers more routes in parallel;
- the constant of proportionality is the **resistivity** $\rho$ of the material, with SI unit **ohm-metre** ($\Omega\,\text{m}$).

Always convert millimetres and millimetres-squared into metres and metres-squared before substituting: $1\ \text{mm}^2 = 1 \times 10^{-6}\ \text{m}^2$.
